  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 236 PM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nConfidence is high that a longwave ridge is expected to influence \nthe county warning area (CWA) Thursday through Saturday morning. \nEnsemble guidance is in agreement of the ridge axis positioning over \nthe Missouri River Valley placing the Tri-State area in southwest \nflow aloft. This feature should amplify a bit then move off to the \neast through Saturday night, bringing zonal westerly flow through at \nleast next Sunday. A weak 500 mb jet accompanies this ridge but \nexits the region by Saturday. Surface low pressure forms Friday, \ninfluencing the area through the end of next weekend. Medium \nconfidence exists of a frontal boundary impacting the area Thursday \nushering in Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) chances heading into \nthe weekend.\n\nWith respect to PoPs, rain is likely (60-80%) early Thursday as the \nfront moves to the east. Rain chances for the afternoon/evening drop \noff a bit but persist with 30-50% chances for the CWA. Friday's \nchances sit at around 25-40% as the system exits our CWA. \nPrecipitation is unlikely Saturday as the upper-level pattern \nsettles into zonal flow. Finally, Sunday presents a slight chance of \nrain (20-30%) during the evening as a shortwave passes through. \nDewpoints are forecasted in the upper 50s/low 60s so ample moisture \nwill be present for rain and thundestorms. Model guidance does \nproject a low instability, low shear environment during the four-day \nforecast period though so confidence in severe weather is low at \nthis point in time.\n\nHigh temperatures are expected to be near the seasonal average for \nthis time of the year for the Tri-State area heading into the \nweekend. Highs in the mid 70s to mid 80s are likely for the entire \nforecast period. Saturday/Sunday should be a bit warmer (low to mid \n80s) than Thursday and Friday (mid to upper 70s). Surface winds \nshould be modest for Thursday and Friday blowing in at 5-15 mph from \nthe southeast, gusting up to 20 mph. Winds Saturday are forecasted \nfrom the south at 5-10 mph gusting to 15 mph. Last but not least, \nSunday's winds are anticipated to be 10-15 mph from the southeast, \ngusting up to 20 mph.",
